function COMPONENT(request : dval) {
    print("<div class=\"doc-detail-layout\"><main class=\"content doc-page doc-detail\"><h2>3. Types</h2><div class=\"doc-section content\"><p>Every Capy value has a type. The compiler checks types before the program runs. It usually infers the type of a local value.</p><h2>Integers</h2><p>An integer literal has type <code>s32</code> when no other type is stated:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var answer := 42\n    print(answer, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p><strong>Output</strong></p><pre><code class=\"language-text\">42\n</code></pre><p>State a wider type on the local, or construct the value explicitly:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var signed : s64 = 8\n    var inferred_signed := s64(9)\n    var unsigned : u64 = 10\n    var inferred_unsigned := u64(11)\n    print(signed, &quot; &quot;, inferred_signed, &quot; &quot;, unsigned, &quot; &quot;, inferred_unsigned, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>Both forms produce the stated wide integer type. <code>var signed : s64 = s64(8)</code> also compiles, but the constructor repeats information already supplied by the annotation.</p><p>Parameters, function results, assignments, typed arrays, struct fields, and the other operand in an expression can also state the integer type. Thus, a function that accepts <code>u64</code> can receive the plain literal <code>8</code>. An unconstrained literal remains <code>s32</code>.</p><p>Arithmetic requires operands of the same type. Use a constructor when the surrounding code does not state the required type.</p><h2>Booleans and floating-point numbers</h2><p>A <code>bool</code> is either <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Conditions require a boolean value.</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : d")
    print("val) {\n    var enabled := true\n    var ratio := 2.5\n    print(enabled, &quot; &quot;, ratio, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>A decimal point or exponent makes an <code>f64</code> literal.</p><h2>Strings</h2><p>A <code>string</code> stores bytes:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := &quot;Ada&quot;\n    var empty := &quot;&quot;\n    print(name, empty, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>An empty string is still a string value. It is not missing data.</p><h2>Arrays, tuples, and structs</h2><p>An array contains values of one type:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&quot;]\n    print(names[0], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>A tuple has a fixed number of positions, which can have different types:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := (3, 4)\n    print(point[0], &quot; &quot;, point[1], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>A struct gives names to fixed fields:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, &quot; &quot;, point.y, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>Arrays and struct instances are mutable reference values inside one workspace. Assignment shares their identity. Tuples remain immutable.</p><p>The collections chapter covers these types in detail.</p><h2>Dynamic values and none</h2><p>A <code>dval</code> holds data whose shape is known only at run time. The <code>none</code> value represents missing dynamic data:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: &quot;Ada&quot;}\n    var missing := none\n    print(profile.name, &quot; &qu")
    print("ot;, missing?, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p><code>none</code> is different from <code>&quot;&quot;</code>, <code>false</code>, an empty map, and an empty list. A <code>dval</code> is also a mutable reference value inside one workspace. The dynamic-values chapter explains mutation, safe reads, and conversions.</p><h2>Type aliases</h2><p>A type alias gives another name to a type:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">type Count = s64\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total : Count = 12\n    print(total, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>An alias does not create a new runtime type.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var answer := 42\n    print(answer, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div><p><strong>Output</strong></p><div class=\"example-output\"><div class=\"example-output-label\">Output</div><pre>42\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var signed : s64 = 8\n    var inferred_signed := s64(9)\n    var unsigned : u64 = 10\n    var inferred_unsigned := u64(11)\n    print(signed, &quot; &quot;, inferred_signed, &quot; &quot;, unsigned, &quot; &quot;, inferred_unsigned, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var enabled := true\n    var ratio := 2.5\n    print(enabled, &quot; &quot;, ratio, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">fu")
    print("nction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := &quot;Ada&quot;\n    var empty := &quot;&quot;\n    print(name, empty, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&quot;]\n    print(names[0], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := (3, 4)\n    print(point[0], &quot; &quot;, point[1], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, &quot; &quot;, point.y, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: &quot;Ada&quot;}\n    var missing := none\n    print(profile.name, &quot; &quot;, missing?, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">type Count = s64\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total : Count = 12\n    print(total, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><nav class=\"guide-navigation\" aria-label=\"Capy guide navigation\"><div class=\"guide-previous\">Previous: <a href=\"/doc/\">Guide index</a></div><div class=\"guide-index\"><a href=\"/doc/\">Guide index</a></div><div class=\"guide-next\">Next: <a hr")
    print("ef=\"/doc/\">Guide index</a></div></nav></main><aside class=\"doc-section see-also\"><h3>See also</h3><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/expressions-and-control-flow/\">4. Expressions and control flow</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/collections-and-records/\">7. Arrays, tuples, and structs</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/dynamic-values/\">8. Dynamic values</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/dvalue/\">dvalue</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/bool/\">bool</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/s32/\">s32</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/s64/\">s64</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/u64/\">u64</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/f64/\">f64</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/string/\">string</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/dval/\">dval</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/length/\">length</a></aside></div>")
}
